Highly Efficient Electrochemical Reduction of $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ to $\mathbf{CH_{4}}$ in Ionic Liquid using Metal-Organic Framework Cathode
Highly efficient electrochemical reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ is of great importance, but is challenging. Herein, Zn-1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid metal-organic frameworks (Zn-BTC MOFs) deposited on carbon paper (CP) were used as cathodes in electrochemical reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ using ionic liquids (ILs) as the electrolytes, which was the first work on combination of a MOF electrode and an pure IL electrolyte 10 in the electrochemical reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . It was found that the efficiency of the reaction depended strongly on the morphology of the $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOFs. Compared with the commonly used metal electrodes, the electrochemical reaction showed much higher selectivity to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and current density, and the overpotentials for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ is much lower. The excellent combination of the MOF cathodes and ILs opens a way for reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ effectively.
## 15 Introduction
Electrocatalysis combines the advantages of efficient conversion of electrical energy into chemical energy with the convenience and stability of heterogeneous catalysis, which has received much attention.1 Transformation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ into useful 20 chemicals and fuels is very attractive because it is a cheap and renewable carbon source, but is very difficult because $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is thermodynamically stable and kinetically inert.2 Electrochemical reduction is a promising method in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ transformation.3 It can be transformed into various products, such as CO, acids, alcohols 25 and hydrocarbons.4 Electrochemical reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ is an alternative route for synthesizing energy-rich and clean fuels, which however currently suffers from low activity and poor selectivity.5
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) represent a class of 30 hybrid materials comprised of ordered networks formed via combining metal ions with organic ligands. MOFs are widely studied for gas storage and capture,7 separation,8 drug delivery and catalysis.10 In addition, MOFs have been used as efficient electrodes in fuel cell systems11 and reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in aqueous 35 or organic electrolytes.12
Ionic liquids (ILs) have attracted considerable attention owing to their unique properties, such as low melting point, negligible vapor pressure, high ionic conductivity, high chemical stability, and adjustable physical and chemical properties.13 40 Applications of ILs in different fields have been studied extensively,14 including those in material synthesis15 and electrochemistry as electrolytes.16
The electrodes and electrolytes are crucial in electrocatalysis, and different electrodes and electrolytes can induce different 45 products. Exploring innovative combination of catalysts and electrolytes is a very interesting topic of great importance. Herein, we conducted the first work on the electrochemical
reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in MOF electrode/IL electrolyte system. It was found that the combination of MOF electrodes and ILs was very $^{50}$ effective for the electrochemical reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , and the morphology of MOFs and the properties of the ILs affected the current density and selectivity to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ significantly.
