In order to confirm the electrocatalytic response shown in Fig. 4a, the controlled potential electrolysis (CPE) experiments 25 were carried out. The electrolysis device is shown schematically in Fig. S9. The current densities using different ${\bf Z}\mathrm{n-MOF/CP}$ cathodes are exhibited in Fig. 4b, which shows that the current densities did not decrease with time in the electrolysis, suggesting that the $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF electrode and the IL were stable. The CV and 30 CPE using CP as cathode were also studied (Fig. S10). The CP cathode produced much lower current density than $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn-MOF/CP}}$ cathode. The $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn-MOF/CP}}$ cathodes prepared from the sheet-like $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF synthesized at $\scriptstyle1=0.38$ generated highest current density due to its largest electroactive surface area. Therefore, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ 35 reduction using this cathode was further studied, and the results are discussed in the following.
After electrolysis of 2 hrs at $-2.2\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. $\mathrm{Ag/Ag^{+}}$ , the gaseous product in the headspace was collected and analyzed by gas chromatography (GC), and the liquid mixture was analyzed by 40 ${}^{1}\mathrm{H}\cdot$ -NMR to quantify liquid products (Fig. S11). There was no product found in the liquid phase. $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ was the dominate product in the gas phase with small amount of CO and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . High selectivity to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ is very difficult to realize using conventional electrolysis systems. We also conducted the electrolysis under 45 different potentials, and the amount of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ $(\mathrm{A_{CH_{4}}})$ is shown in Fig. 5a. It can be clearly seen that $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ production rate increased dramatically at the potentials less negative than $-2.2\mathrm{~V~}$ vs $\mathrm{Ag/Ag^{+}}$ , and rose very slowly at the potentials more negative than $-2.2\mathrm{V}$ vs $\mathrm{Ag/Ag^{+}}$ . Therefore, electrolysis under $-2.2\mathrm{V}$ vs $\mathrm{Ag/Ag^{+}}$ 50 was most suitable for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ production. $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ began to generate at - $1.95\mathrm{~V~}$ from extrapolation method using the current densities for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ under different potentials (Fig. 5b). The distinct pre-feature (Fig. 4a) at the potential less negative than $-1.95\mathrm{~V~}$ vs $\mathrm{Ag/Ag^{+}}$ was originated mainly from the generation of CO. The 55 overpotential for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ was $0.25\mathrm{~V~}$ for this process at $-2.2\mathrm{~V~}$ vs $\mathrm{Ag/Ag^{+}}$ with the current density of $3.1\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . Moreover, the data from Tafel plot (Fig. 4c), which was obtained by electrolysis voltage, was linear in the range of $\scriptstyle\mathfrak{\eta=}0.19\ –0.37\ \mathrm{V}$ with the Tafel
slope of $146\ \mathrm{mV\cdotdecade^{-1}}$ , indicating a rate-determining initial 60 electron transfer to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to form an adsorbed $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\cdot$ - intermediate.20 XPS spectra of the $Z_{\mathrm{n-MOF}}$ were given before and after the electrolysis in Fig. S4. The results demonstrated that the spectra did not change notably, further indicating the excellent stability of the $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn-MOF/CP}}$ cathode. In addition, the 65 electrolyte was also stable according to the $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ -NMR spectra (Fig. S11).
