has strong interaction with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ .24 The viscosities of the ILs also affected the $j_{\mathrm{tot}}.$ , as can be known from Table 1. We also 15 conducted the electrolysis using the $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF electrode combined with other electrolytes, including DMF containing 0.01 M tetrabutylammonium tetrafluoroborate $\mathrm{(TBABF_{4,}}$ , MeCN containing 0.1 M tetrabutylammonium hexafluorophosphate $\left(\mathrm{TBAPF}_{6}\right)$ , and MeCN containing $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ $\mathrm{BmimBF_{4}}$ , and results 20 are listed in Table S3. The results show that the Faradaic efficiency for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ was very low when these electrolytes were used, indicating that imidazolium based ILs were crucial for the very high selectivity to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ .
Metal electrodes are commonly used to reduce $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . In this work, we carried out the electrolysis using Au, Ag, Pt, Fe, Zn, 30 and $\mathrm{Cu}$ cathodes in $\mathrm{BmimBF_{4}}$ at different voltages as well. The metal electrodes used were polished metal foils with bulk structure, therefore the effect of the surface structure can be ignored. The $j_{\mathrm{tot}}$ and partial current densities of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}~(j_{\mathrm{CH}4})$ , CO $(j_{\mathrm{{CO}}})$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\ (j_{\mathrm{H}2})$ over the potential range from $-1.9\mathrm{~V~}$ to $-2.5\mathrm{~V~}$ 35 vs $\mathrm{{Ag/Ag}^{+}}$ are compared with the results obtained from the $Z\mathrm{n}$ - MOF cathode in Fig. 7. The CVs of different metal cathodes are shown in Fig. S12, and the principal product, linear range in Tafel plot and Tafel slope for their main product are provided in Table S4. Obviously, the $j_{t o t}$ and the selectivity to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ of the $Z\mathrm{n\cdot}$ - 40 MOF/CP system were much larger than that of the metal cathode systems at the same voltage, suggesting that the Zn-MOF electrode was also very important for the high efficiency in producing $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . The potential was less negative using ZnMOF/CP cathode than metal cathodes to reach the same current 45 density of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ , indicating that the Zn-MOF/CP cathode was more active for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ generation. For comparison, Table S5 lists the $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ selectivity reported in the literature for the electrochemical reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . The data indicate that the selectivity to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ in our work was the highest. The product 50 contained $85\%$ CO and $15\%\mathrm{H}_{2}$ when CP was used as the cathode in $\mathrm{BmimBF_{4}}$ at $-2.2\mathrm{~V~}$ vs $\mathrm{Ag/Ag^{+}}$ , further indicating that the $Z\mathrm{n}$ - MOFs played a key role for producing $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . These results demonstrate that the ${\bf Z}{\bf n{-}}{\bf M O F/C P}$ cathode and the I $\mathrm{\Omega},\mathrm{BmimBF_{4}}$ are excellent combination for producing $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ from 55 electrochemical reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ .
As discussed above, both $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF cathodes and imidazolium based $\mathrm{IL}$ electrolytes are crucial for the high yield of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . The ${\cal Z}\mathrm{n-MOF/CP}$ cathodes and imidazolium based $\mathrm{IL}$ electrolytes are excellent combination for producing $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . In addition, the current 60 density reached the highest value using ${\bf Z}\mathrm{n-MOF/CP}$ cathode prepared using $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF synthesized at $\mathrm{\Phi}_{\mathrm{X=0}.38}$ in ${\mathrm{BmimBF}}_{4}$ .
The high electrochemical activity of the Zn-MOFs in $\mathrm{BmimBF_{4}}$ results partially from the facts that the imidazolium based ILs containing fluorine can absorb and activate $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ ,24 and 70 the $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOFs are porous materials, which benefits gas adsorption.7 The $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOFs were synthesized in imidazolium based IL mixture, which therefore has very good compatibility with  ILs, which helps driving the reaction.16 In addition, it is reported that Zn-MOFs are efficient selective adsorbent for 75 different gases.17 At present, there is no method to determine the gas adsorption amount on solid surface in the presence of a liquid, so we determined the adsorption amounts of the gases in the absence of IL in order to get some indirect evidence to discuss the interactions between the gases and the electrode, and the 80 adsorption properties of the $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , CO, $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ (Fig. 8) were studied. The adsorption amounts of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , CO, and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ on the $Z_{\mathrm{n-MOF}}$ at 1 atm and $298\mathrm{~K~}$ are $9.7~\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , 3.9 $\mathrm{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , and $1.0~\mathrm{cm}^{3}\cdot\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, indicating that $Z\mathrm{n-MOF}$ had much stronger adsorption for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and CO than for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . Furthermore, only CO can be detected at the potential less negative than $-1.95\mathrm{~V~}$ vs $\mathrm{\Ag/Ag^{+}}$ , and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ began to generate at - 5 $1.95\mathrm{~V~}$ and became dominate product soon (Fig. 5b), suggesting that $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ was derived from CO. $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ was absorbed on the surface of $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOFs and was reduced to CO. Most of the CO molecules generated tended to be further reduced to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ because the interaction between $Z_{\mathrm{n-MOF}}$ and CO is stronger than that 10 between $Z_{\mathrm{n-MOF}}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ .
On the basis of the results of this work and the knowledge in 15 the literature, the possible pathway for electrochemical reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ using ${\cal Z}\mathrm{n-MOF/CP}$ cathodes in imidazolium based ILs can be discussed briefly, which is shown schematically in Fig. 9. In the electrolysis, some imidazolium cations were adsorbed on the $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF surface after the Zn-MOF/CP cathode immersed in 20 the IL electrolyte. Thus, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules were captured by the IL adsorbed on the $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF surface. One electron was transferred to a $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecule and forms $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{^{\cdot\cdot}}$ intermediate, and then the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ ˙ intermediate took another electron and yields a CO molecule. In this step, the conductive $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF transferred electron to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , 25 and imidazolium base ILs helped driving the transformation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{^{\cdot\cdot}}$ intermediate. CO could be desorbed from the surface of $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF or be further reduced by six electrons to generate $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ .4d Due to the larger adsorption capacity of CO than $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ on the ${\cal Z}\mathrm{n-MOF}$ as discussed above, the CO molecule 30 preferred to be adsorbed on the $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF surface to be further reduced to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . More detailed mechanism is very interesting, but is challenging.
In summary, the ${\cal Z}\mathrm{n-MOF/CP}$ cathodes and $\mathrm{IL}$ electrolytes have been combined for electrochemical reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ The morphology of the $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOFs has significant effect on the electrochemical reaction. The sheet-like $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF has the highest 40 activity in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction due to its largest electroactive surface areas, and the imidazolium based ILs with fluorine are more effective electrolytes because fluorine has stronger interaction with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . The $Z_{\mathrm{n-MOF}}$ based cathodes and the ILs are excellent combination for the efficient and selective reduction 45 of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . The selectivity of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ can be higher than $80\%$ at a current density of higher than $3\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ with an overpotentail of $0.25\mathrm{~V~}$ . We believe that integration of MOF-based electrodes and ILs provides many opportunities for exploring efficient electrochemical reactions.
