## Materials characterization
XRD analysis of the samples was performed on the X-ray diffractometer (Model D/MAX2500, Rigaka) with $\mathrm{Cu-Ka}$ 5 radiation, and the scan speed was $2^{\circ}/\mathrm{min}$ . The surface morphologies of the products were characterized by a HITACHI S-4800 SEM. The surface components were characterized by XPS performed on the Thermo Scientific ESCALab 250Xi using $200~\mathrm{W}$ monochromated Al $\mathrm{K}\mathfrak{a}$ radiation, in which the $500~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ X10 ray spot was used. The adsorption isotherms of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , CO and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ of the degassed $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOFs were determined at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ in the pressure range of 0-1 atm on a TriStar II 3020 device.
## Fabrication of Zn-MOF/CP cathodes and characterization
The EPD procedure was performed on DC power supply 15 LW6020KD (Longwei Instrument (HK) Co. Ltd). Prior to experiments, the Carbon paper (CP) substrate was sonicated in acetone for $10~\mathrm{min}$ , followed by washing with water and ethanol, and finally dried in ${\bf N}_{2}$ atmosphere. The procedures were similar to that reported.19 For each series of EPD experiments, $1\ \mathrm{mg\Zn}.$ - 20 MOF powder was dispersed in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ methanol/DMF solution (the concentration of DMF was $20\ \mathrm{\wt\%}$ ) prior to $10~\mathrm{\min}$ sonication. Then two CP substrates of the same size $0.5\mathrm{cm}\times0.5$ cm) were used as anode and cathode, respectively. The $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF was deposited onto anode by applying a suitable constant voltage 25 $(10–50\mathrm{~V})$ of 1 hour, and the distance between electrodes was 2 cm. The as-deposited Zn-MOF films were washed with ethanol for several times and dried in vacuum oven at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24~\mathrm{{hrs}}$ . The morphologies of the as-synthesized ${\cal Z}\mathrm{n-MOF/CP}$ cathodes were characterized by SEM.
