## Electrochemical surface area measurement
The active surface areas in the IL may be different with that determined in the aqueous solution. However, the acceptable characterization method in ILs has not been reported. Therefore, 60 in this work, we made the characterization using the wellaccepted method as follows.11 The electrochemical surface areas of the ${\bf Z}\mathrm{n-MOF/CP}$ electrodes were determined by steady-state cyclic voltammetry (CV) in a solution of 0.01M [Fe $\left(\mathrm{CN}\right)_{6}]^{3-4}$ with $1\mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{KCl}$ at a scan rate of $50\ \mathrm{mV^{.}s^{-1}}$ . The electrochemical 65 surface area was estimated according to the Randles-Sevcik equation.27
## EIS measurements
The measurement was performed using the Zn-MOF/CP electrodes as the reference.28 The experimental apparatus were 70 the same as for CV measurements. The impedance spectra was recorded in IL $\mathrm{.\BmimBF_{4}}$ at an open circuit potential (OCP) with an amplitude of $5~\mathrm{mV}$ of $10^{-2}$ to $10^{5}\mathrm{Hz}$ . The formal potential of the system was also set at $-2.0\mathrm{~V~}$ similar to the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction potential at the same experimental conditions. The data obtained 75 from the EIS measurements were fitted using the Zview software (Version 3.1, Scribner Associates, USA).
## $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ reduction electrolysis and product analysis
Electrolysis was performed under room temperature $(25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ in a commonly used H-type cell with an $\mathrm{Ag/Ag^{+}}(0.01\mathrm{M}\mathrm{AgNO_{3}}$ 80 in $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ TBAP-MeCN) reference electrode, which was similar to that used by other researchers.3,16 The apparatus was shown schematically in Fig. S9. The cathode and anode compartments were separated by a proton exchange membrane (Nafion 117). ILs or organic solvents and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ aqueous solution $(0.5\mathbf{M})$ were 85 used as cathodic and anodic electrolytes, respectively. The proton source was from the electrolysis of water at the anode. Before electrolysis, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ was bubbled through the catholyte $2~\mathrm{mL}$ per min) for $30~\mathrm{\min}$ with stirring. Potentiostatic electrochemical reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ was carried out with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ bubbling $2{\mathrm{~mL~}}$ per $\L_{90}\ \mathrm{min}\L_{\cdot}$ ), and the gaseous product was collected in a gas bag. After a desired electrolysis time, the gaseous product in the gas bag was collected and analyzed by gas chromatography (GC, HP 4890D), which was equipped with TCD and FID detectors using helium as the internal standard, and the liquid mixture was analyzed by $^1\mathrm{H}$ - 95 NMR method, which recorded on a Bruker Avance III $400~\mathrm{HD}$ spectrometer in DMSO- ${\bf d}_{6}$ with TMS as an internal standard. The amount of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and Faradaic efficiency of the products were calculated on the basis of GC analysis.3b,29
Multiple electrolysis experiments were run at each potential 100 and the average current density was calculated to give the data. The products of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction vs hydrogen reduction were measured at each potential. The variation in partial current density vs applied overpotential was obtained via stepped potential electrolysis. Partial current densities for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ production 105 were calculated from the GC spectra every 15 minutes and averaged over 1-2 hours. The Tafel plots were constructed from these data.
